export const R1={title:'Pharmacy practice, careers and patient screening (Skill V)',hook:'A patient at the counter says her new prescription made her itchy. Another wants a flu shot. A third is two weeks late picking up his blood pressure refill. The pharmacy technician is often the first person who hears these concerns. This lesson introduces pharmacy practice, the event and the screening conversations that Skill V asks you to lead in four minutes.',objectives:['Describe the Pharmacy Science event, its test plan and its seven skills.','Trace key moments in pharmacy history and compare practice settings.','Distinguish the roles of pharmacists and pharmacy technicians.','Conduct a patient screening using a memorized checklist and refer the patient to the pharmacist.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Pharmacy Science has two rounds. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes**; HOSA provides a basic calculator. The test plan is: pharmacy history and practice settings **4%**, law, ethics, safety and infection control **10%**, terminology and abbreviations **10%**, dosage forms and routes **10%**, sterile and non-sterile compounding **10%**, concentrations and dilutions **10%** and **disorders and treatments 46%**. Nearly half the test is about diseases and the drugs used to treat them, so that is where most study time should go.',
'Top scorers advance to **Round Two**, a skills assessment drawn from seven skills: **I** identifying instruments and equipment (15 minutes; may be given with the test), **II** verifying a controlled-substance prescription and DEA number (6), **III** withdrawing liquid from a vial (15), **IV** compounding an oral suspension (15), **V** patient screening (4), **VI** filling a prescription (5) and **VII** aseptic garbing, hand washing and gloving (10). Scenarios may combine skills, and you must complete or verbalize every step. You need **70%** on the Round Two skills (excluding the test and Skill I) to be recognized; ties go to the higher test score.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Area','Weight'],rows:[['Disorders and treatments','46%'],['Law and ethics; safety and infection control','10%'],['Terminology and abbreviations','10%'],['Dosage forms and routes','10%'],['Sterile and non-sterile compounding','10%'],['Concentrations and dilutions','10%'],['History and practice settings','4%']]},
{t:'callout',kind:'exam',title:'Use of zero',text:'The guidelines require a **leading zero** before a decimal less than 1 (write 0.5 mg, never .5 mg) and **no trailing zero** after a whole number (write 5 mg, never 5.0 mg). A missed decimal point can cause a tenfold overdose.'}]],
['A short history of pharmacy',[
'People have prepared medicines from plants, minerals and animals for thousands of years. Ancient Egyptian papyri list hundreds of remedies, and the Greek physician **Galen** described methods of mixing drugs that gave us the word **galenical**. In 1240, the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II issued an edict separating pharmacy from medicine in his kingdom—the idea that the person who prescribes should not also be the person who prepares and sells. The first U.S. college of pharmacy opened in Philadelphia in 1821, and the **United States Pharmacopeia (USP)** published its first set of drug standards in 1820.',
'In the 20th century, mass manufacturing replaced most hand compounding, and laws gave the government power to require that drugs be safe and effective. Pharmacy shifted from making drugs toward **patient care**: checking prescriptions for safety, counseling patients, giving vaccines and managing chronic disease. Today’s pharmacist earns a **Doctor of Pharmacy (PharmD)** degree, and technicians handle many technical tasks so pharmacists can focus on clinical decisions.',
order('Put these milestones in order',['Galen describes compounding methods','Frederick II separates pharmacy from medicine (1240)','First USP drug standards (1820)','First U.S. college of pharmacy (1821)','Federal law requires proof of drug safety (1938)','PharmD becomes the entry degree for pharmacists'])]],
['Practice settings and roles',[
'**Community (retail) pharmacies** fill prescriptions, sell over-the-counter products and give vaccines. **Hospital (institutional) pharmacies** prepare unit doses, IV admixtures and emergency medications for inpatients. Other settings include **long-term care**, **home infusion**, **mail-order**, **nuclear pharmacy** (radioactive drugs for imaging), **compounding pharmacies**, **specialty pharmacies** for expensive biologic drugs, the **pharmaceutical industry**, the military and the Indian Health Service.',
'**Pharmacists** evaluate prescriptions, check for interactions and allergies, counsel patients and make clinical judgments. **Pharmacy technicians** work under the pharmacist’s supervision: they take in prescriptions, enter data, count and label, compound, manage inventory and billing and screen patients. Technicians **refer** clinical questions—such as “Can I take this with my other medicine?”—to the pharmacist. Many states require technician registration, and national certification is available through the **PTCB** (CPhT) or the **NHA** (ExCPT).',
sort('Pharmacist or technician?',['Pharmacist responsibility','Technician can do (under supervision)'],[['Counseling a patient about side effects',0],['Counting tablets for a prescription',1],['Deciding whether an interaction is significant',0],['Entering a prescription into the computer',1],['Ordering stock from a wholesaler',1],['Making the final check of a filled prescription',0]])]],
['Screening conversations',[
'In **Skill V** you screen a patient so the pharmacist has the information needed for consultation or a service such as a vaccine. You greet the patient, introduce yourself, provide **privacy**, record the patient’s **name and date of birth** to verify identity and ask **five questions** from a screening checklist you have **memorized**—the checklist is not provided at the event. Questions do not need to be asked in order. You then sign the screening form, **refer the patient to the pharmacist** and hand the form to the pharmacist (judge).',
'Communication earns many of the points: no distracting nonverbal behavior (keep eye contact and an open posture), **no medical jargon**, appropriate volume and pronunciation, **active listening** without interrupting and follow-up questions to make sure you understand. A “yes” answer on a vaccine screening does **not** automatically mean the vaccine is withheld—it means the pharmacist needs more information.',
order('Skill V flow',['Greet the patient and introduce yourself','Provide privacy','Record name and date of birth','Ask the five memorized screening questions','Clarify answers you do not fully understand','Sign the screening form','Refer the patient to the pharmacist','Hand the form to the pharmacist'])]],
['The four screening checklists',[
'HOSA lists four checklists. **Inactivated poliovirus (IPV) vaccine:** allergies to any vaccine component; whether the person is immune-depressed; whether they live with someone with an immune-deficiency disease; suspected familial immune deficiency; possibility of pregnancy. **Rubella vaccine:** allergies to any vaccine component; immunosuppression; receiving corticosteroids; diagnosed with tuberculosis; possibility of pregnancy or trying to become pregnant.',
'**Late refill (adherence):** how the prescriber recommended taking it; methods used to remember doses; side effects experienced; whether the medicine helped the condition; any other reasons for not taking it as prescribed. **Possible allergic reaction:** which medication they were taking when symptoms began; a description of symptoms; when symptoms began; other medications taken at the same time, including OTC drugs; whether they have stopped taking the medication. Severe symptoms—trouble breathing, throat or face swelling—need emergency care.',
flip('Checklist recall',[['IPV vaccine','Allergies, immune-depressed, lives with immune deficiency, familial immune deficiency, pregnancy'],['Rubella vaccine','Allergies, immunosuppressed, corticosteroids, tuberculosis, pregnancy or trying'],['Late refill','How prescribed, reminders, side effects, did it help, other reasons'],['Allergic reaction','Which drug, symptoms, when began, other drugs incl. OTC, stopped it?'],['A “yes” answer','Means more information is needed—not an automatic no'],['Last step','Hand the signed form to the pharmacist']])]],
['Practice a screening',[
'Plain language helps patients answer honestly. Instead of “Are you immunocompromised?” ask “Do you have any condition or take any medicine that weakens your immune system?” Instead of “Are you adherent?” ask “How often do you miss a dose?” Repeat key answers back to the patient to confirm them, and write each answer clearly on the form next to the question topic.',
kase('Late refill screening',{a:{text:'Mr. Ortiz is 12 days late refilling his lisinopril. He seems embarrassed. How do you begin? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Move to a private area and ask, “How did your doctor tell you to take this medicine?”','b','Correct. Privacy and a neutral, open question.',true],['Say loudly, “You are noncompliant with your medication.”','b','That is jargon, not private and sounds judgmental.',false]]},b:{text:'He says it makes him cough, so he skips it. What next?',choices:[['Note the side effect, finish the remaining questions and refer him to the pharmacist','c','Correct. The pharmacist evaluates the side effect.',true],['Tell him to stop taking it','c','Technicians do not give clinical advice.',false]]},c:{text:'You sign the form, refer him to the pharmacist and hand the form over. Skill complete.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Round One: 50 questions in 60 minutes, with disorders and treatments 46%; Round Two draws on seven skills and requires 70% on the skills.','Pharmacy moved from compounding to patient care; pharmacists make clinical decisions, technicians perform technical tasks under supervision and refer clinical questions.','Skill V: greet, privacy, name and DOB, five memorized checklist questions, no jargon, active listening, sign, refer and hand the form to the pharmacist.'],terms:[['PharmD','Doctor of Pharmacy, the degree required to become a pharmacist.'],['CPhT','Certified Pharmacy Technician credential from the PTCB.'],['Adherence','Taking a medication as prescribed.']],mcqs:[q('What percent of the Pharmacy Science test covers disorders and treatments?','46%','10%','4%','25%','Nearly half the test.'),q('How long is the Skill V patient screening?','4 minutes','15 minutes','10 minutes','6 minutes','Short and focused.'),q('Who makes the final check of a filled prescription?','The pharmacist','The technician','The patient','The cashier','Technicians submit for final check.'),q('Which question belongs on the rubella vaccine screening checklist?','Is the person receiving corticosteroids?','How do you remember to take your medicine?','When did your symptoms begin?','Do you live with anyone who has an immune deficiency disease?','The last option is on the IPV list.'),q('What does a “yes” on a vaccine screening question mean?','More information is needed before deciding','The vaccine is never given','The patient must leave','Nothing','The pharmacist evaluates it.'),q('How is a value of one-half milligram written correctly?','0.5 mg','.5 mg','0.50 mg','½mg','Use a leading zero.'),q('Which setting prepares IV admixtures for inpatients?','Hospital pharmacy','Mail-order pharmacy','Nuclear pharmacy only','Retail cashier','Institutional practice.'),q('In Skill V, after signing the screening form you should:','Refer the patient to the pharmacist and hand over the form','Give the patient advice on stopping the drug','Throw the form away','Call the prescriber yourself','Final steps.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would conduct a Skill V screening for a patient with a possible allergic reaction, including the five questions and the communication behaviors judges look for.',rubric:[['Opening and identity',['greet','introduce','privacy','name','date of birth']],['Five allergy questions',['which medication','symptoms','when','other medications','otc','stopped']],['Communication',['eye contact','jargon','listen','interrupt','clarify','volume']],['Closing and scope',['sign','refer','pharmacist','form','emergency']]],model:'I greet the patient, introduce myself and move to a private area or say that I am providing privacy. I record the patient’s name and date of birth on the screening form to verify identity. I then ask the five allergy questions in plain language: which medication were you taking when you noticed the symptoms; please describe the symptoms; when did they begin; did you take any other medications, including over-the-counter products, during this time; and have you stopped taking the medication. I keep eye contact and an open posture, avoid medical jargon, speak clearly, listen without interrupting and ask follow-up questions or repeat answers back so I understand them completely. If the patient describes trouble breathing or swelling of the face or throat, I recognize that as an emergency. I record each answer beside the question, sign the form, refer the patient to the pharmacist for counseling and hand the completed form to the pharmacist, because evaluating the reaction is the pharmacist’s clinical decision.'},sources:[RULES,ASHP_TECH,BLS_RX,CDC_CONTRA,AAAAI_DRUG]};

export const R2={title:'Pharmacy law, ethics, safety and DEA verification (Skill II)',hook:'In 1937, more than 100 people died after taking an “elixir” of sulfanilamide dissolved in a chemical similar to antifreeze. No law required the company to test it for safety. The next year Congress passed the Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act. Pharmacy law is a history of lessons learned. This lesson covers the major laws, controlled substances and how to verify a DEA number for Skill II.',objectives:['Identify major federal drug laws and the problems they addressed.','Describe the five controlled-substance schedules and refill rules.','Verify all required parts of a controlled-substance prescription and check a DEA number.','Apply ethics, safety and infection-control principles in the pharmacy.'],sections:[
['Major federal drug laws',[
'The **Pure Food and Drug Act (1906)** banned adulterated and misbranded drugs. The **Harrison Narcotic Tax Act (1914)** regulated opium and coca products. The **Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (1938)** required manufacturers to prove **safety** before marketing and prohibited false therapeutic claims; it created the modern FDA framework. The **Durham-Humphrey Amendment (1951)** separated **prescription (legend)** drugs from **over-the-counter** drugs and allowed oral and refill prescriptions. The **Kefauver-Harris Amendment (1962)**, passed after thalidomide caused birth defects in Europe, required proof of **effectiveness** as well as safety.',
'The **Controlled Substances Act (1970)** created the drug schedules and DEA registration. The **Poison Prevention Packaging Act (1970)** requires **child-resistant containers** unless the patient or prescriber requests otherwise. The **Orphan Drug Act (1983)** encouraged drugs for rare diseases. The **Hatch-Waxman Act (1984)** created the modern generic approval pathway. **OBRA ’90** required prospective drug review and an **offer to counsel** Medicaid patients. **HIPAA (1996)** protects health information. The **Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act (2005)** limits pseudoephedrine sales, and the **Drug Quality and Security Act (2013)** addressed compounding and drug supply-chain tracing.',
match('Match the law to its main effect',[['Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (1938)','Drugs must be proven safe'],['Durham-Humphrey (1951)','Prescription versus OTC drugs'],['Kefauver-Harris (1962)','Drugs must be proven effective'],['Controlled Substances Act (1970)','Drug schedules and DEA registration'],['Poison Prevention Packaging Act (1970)','Child-resistant containers'],['OBRA ’90','Drug review and offer to counsel'],['Hatch-Waxman (1984)','Generic drug approval pathway'],['Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act (2005)','Limits on pseudoephedrine sales']])]],
['Controlled substances',[
'The DEA places drugs with abuse potential into five **schedules**. **Schedule I** drugs have high abuse potential and no accepted medical use in the U.S. (for example, heroin). **Schedule II** drugs have high abuse potential with accepted medical use—oxycodone, hydrocodone combination products, morphine, fentanyl, methylphenidate and amphetamine salts. **Schedule III** includes products such as testosterone and acetaminophen with codeine; **Schedule IV** includes benzodiazepines such as alprazolam and sleep drugs such as zolpidem; **Schedule V** includes some cough syrups with small amounts of codeine and pregabalin.',
'Schedule II prescriptions **cannot be refilled**; a new prescription is needed each time (emergency oral orders have strict follow-up rules). Schedule III and IV prescriptions may be refilled up to **5 times within 6 months** of the date written. Schedule II stock is ordered with **DEA Form 222** (or its electronic equivalent), theft or significant loss is reported on **DEA Form 106**, and inventories are kept under federal and state rules. Technicians must follow pharmacy procedures exactly because controlled-substance diversion is a serious crime.',
sort('Which schedule?',['Schedule II','Schedule III','Schedule IV'],[['Oxycodone',0],['Methylphenidate',0],['Acetaminophen with codeine',1],['Testosterone',1],['Alprazolam',2],['Zolpidem',2]])]],
['Verifying a controlled-substance prescription',[
'For **Skill II** you receive a prescription with **five missing components** and complete a Prescription Verification Form in 6 minutes. A controlled-substance prescription must include: **patient information** (name, address, date of birth); **prescriber information** (name, address, telephone number, license number); a **DEA number**; the **date** written (month, day, year); **drug name and strength**; **dose and quantity**; **route**; **directions (sig)**; **number of refills**; whether **product selection (generic substitution)** is permitted; and the **prescriber’s signature**. It must also be free of **dangerous abbreviations** on the Joint Commission Do Not Use lists.',
'If the prescription lacks required elements, the technician does not guess. The usual steps are to **alert the pharmacist**, who **contacts the prescriber** to obtain or verify the missing information, documents the clarification as the law and pharmacy policy allow, and holds the prescription until it is complete. Some elements—such as a missing signature on a Schedule II prescription—cannot be added by phone and require a new, valid prescription.',
flip('Required components',[['Patient','Name, address, date of birth'],['Prescriber','Name, address, phone, license number, DEA number'],['Date','Month, day and year written'],['Drug','Name and strength'],['Amount','Dose and quantity'],['Use','Route and directions (sig)'],['Refills and substitution','Number of refills; product selection permitted?'],['Signature','The prescriber’s signature']])]],
['Checking a DEA number',[
'A DEA number has **two letters and seven digits**. The first letter identifies the registrant type (for example, A, B, F or G for many practitioners and M for mid-level practitioners). The **second letter** should match the **first letter of the prescriber’s last name**. Then use the checksum: **(a)** confirm the second letter; **(b)** add the **1st, 3rd and 5th** digits; **(c)** add the **2nd, 4th and 6th** digits and **double** the sum; **(d)** add the results of b and c; **(e)** the **last digit** of that total must match the **7th digit** of the DEA number.',
'The checksum catches many invented numbers, but it does not prove a prescription is legitimate. Pharmacists also look for red flags such as unusual quantities, prescribers far from the patient’s home, altered handwriting or many patients receiving identical prescriptions, and they can verify registrations with the DEA.',
{t:'example',title:'Is this DEA number valid?',prompt:'Dr. Maya Smith’s prescription lists DEA number AS1234563. (Invented for practice.)',steps:['(a) Second letter S matches Smith. ✔','(b) 1st + 3rd + 5th digits: 1 + 3 + 5 = 9','(c) 2nd + 4th + 6th digits: 2 + 4 + 6 = 12; doubled = 24','(d) 9 + 24 = 33','(e) Last digit of 33 is 3; the 7th digit of the DEA number is 3. ✔'],answer:'Valid by the checksum.'},
{t:'example',title:'Try another',prompt:'Dr. Leo Brown’s prescription lists DEA number FB2468135. (Invented for practice.)',steps:['(a) Second letter B matches Brown. ✔','(b) 2 + 6 + 1 = 9','(c) 4 + 8 + 3 = 15; doubled = 30','(d) 9 + 30 = 39','(e) Last digit 9 does not match the 7th digit, 5. ✘'],answer:'Not valid. Alert the pharmacist.'},
order('DEA check order',['Second letter matches the prescriber’s last name','Add digits 1, 3 and 5','Add digits 2, 4 and 6 and double','Add the two results','Compare the last digit with the 7th digit'])]],
['Dangerous abbreviations',[
'The **Joint Commission “Do Not Use” list** (minimum list) bans: **U** or **u** for unit (write “unit”), **IU** (write “international unit”), **Q.D.** and **Q.O.D.** (write “daily” and “every other day”), a **trailing zero** (write 5 mg, not 5.0 mg), a **missing leading zero** (write 0.5 mg, not .5 mg) and **MS, MSO₄ and MgSO₄** (write “morphine sulfate” or “magnesium sulfate”). Its recommended list adds symbols such as **> and <**, drug-name abbreviations, apothecary units, **@** and **cc** (write mL) and **µg** (write mcg).',
'ISMP keeps a longer list of error-prone abbreviations, including **SC** or **SQ** (write “subcut”), **HS** (could mean half-strength or bedtime) and **OD/OS/OU, AD/AS/AU**, which are easily confused. In Skill II you must say whether any dangerous abbreviations appear and list them.',
sort('Allowed or Do Not Use?',['Acceptable','Do Not Use'],[['10 units',0],['10 U',1],['0.5 mg',0],['.5 mg',1],['5.0 mg',1],['daily',0],['QD',1],['MSO₄',1]])]],
['Ethics, safety and infection control',[
'Pharmacy staff follow codes of ethics that emphasize **patient welfare, honesty, confidentiality** and **respect for autonomy**. Protect health information under HIPAA, never discuss patients where others can overhear and never access records without a work reason. Medication errors should be reported, not hidden; reporting systems such as **FDA MedWatch** and the **ISMP** program help everyone learn. Look-alike, sound-alike names (hydroxyzine and hydralazine) are separated on shelves and may use **tall-man lettering** (hydrOXYzine, hydrALAZINE).',
'Infection control includes hand hygiene, cleaning counting trays and equipment, safe **sharps disposal**, and following USP standards for compounding. **Hazardous drugs** such as many chemotherapy agents are handled under **USP <800>** with special gloves, gowns and ventilated cabinets. Each chemical has a **Safety Data Sheet (SDS)**, and an **eyewash station** must be available for splashes.',
kase('Ethics at the counter',{a:{text:'A neighbor asks you what medicine your mutual friend picked up yesterday. What do you say?',choices:[['“I’m sorry, I can’t share any patient information.”','b','Correct. HIPAA and ethics protect confidentiality.',true],['Tell them, since they are friends','b','That violates privacy.',false]]},b:{text:'You notice you counted the wrong strength into a vial before the final check. What do you do?',choices:[['Tell the pharmacist and fix it; follow the error-reporting policy','c','Correct. Honesty prevents harm.',true],['Quietly swap it and say nothing','c','Errors and near misses should be reported so systems improve.',false]]},c:{text:'The pharmacist thanks you for catching it. Near-miss reporting helps prevent future errors.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Key laws: 1938 FDCA (safety), 1951 Durham-Humphrey (Rx vs OTC), 1962 Kefauver-Harris (efficacy), 1970 CSA and PPPA, 1984 Hatch-Waxman, OBRA ’90, HIPAA.','Schedule II: no refills; III–IV: up to 5 refills in 6 months; a controlled-substance prescription needs patient, prescriber, DEA, date, drug, dose, route, sig, refills, substitution and signature.','DEA check: second letter = last name; (1+3+5) + 2×(2+4+6); last digit must match the 7th digit; avoid Do Not Use abbreviations.'],terms:[['DEA number','A registration number allowing a prescriber to prescribe controlled substances.'],['Legend drug','A drug that requires a prescription.'],['Tall-man lettering','Capital letters used to tell look-alike drug names apart.']],mcqs:[q('Which 1938 law began requiring drugs to be proven safe and prohibited false therapeutic claims?','Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act','Durham-Humphrey Amendment','Harris Narcotic Tax Act','Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which law requires child-resistant containers?','Poison Prevention Packaging Act','Hatch-Waxman Act','Orphan Drug Act','HIPAA','Patients may request an easy-open cap.'),q('How many refills are allowed on a Schedule II prescription?','None','5 within 6 months','Unlimited for 1 year','1','A new prescription is required.'),q('For DEA number BJ3157246 (prescriber Dr. Jones), what is the check result?','Invalid—the check total is 34, and 4 does not match the 7th digit, 6','Valid—the second letter matches Jones','Valid—the check total ends in 6','It cannot be checked without calling the DEA','3 + 5 + 2 = 10; 2 × (1 + 7 + 4) = 24; 10 + 24 = 34.'),q('Which is on the Joint Commission Do Not Use list?','QD','mg','mL','daily','Write “daily.”'),q('Alprazolam is in which schedule?','Schedule IV','Schedule I','Schedule II','Schedule V','Benzodiazepines are C-IV.'),q('Which amendment added a requirement for proof of effectiveness?','Kefauver-Harris','Durham-Humphrey','Pure Food and Drug Act','Controlled Substances Act','Passed after the thalidomide disaster.'),q('A prescription is missing the patient’s date of birth. What should the technician do?','Alert the pharmacist, who clarifies with the prescriber','Write in a guess','Fill it anyway','Throw it away','Never guess at missing information.')],frq:{prompt:'A controlled-substance prescription for Dr. Ana Garcia lists DEA number AG2389417. Show the five steps to check it, state whether it is valid and explain what you would do if required elements were missing. (Number invented for practice.)',rubric:[['Letter check',['second letter','g','garcia','last name']],['Digit sums',['2','8','4','14','3','9','1','13','26']],['Result',['40','0','7','not valid','invalid','match']],['Missing elements',['pharmacist','prescriber','contact','clarify','new prescription','do not guess']]],model:'Step a: the second letter is G, which matches the first letter of Garcia’s last name. Step b: I add the first, third and fifth digits: 2 + 8 + 4 = 14. Step c: I add the second, fourth and sixth digits, 3 + 9 + 1 = 13, and double it to get 26. Step d: 14 + 26 = 40. Step e: the last digit of 40 is 0, but the seventh digit of the DEA number is 7, so the number is not valid. I would not fill the prescription; I would alert the pharmacist. Likewise, if required elements such as the patient’s address, the date written or the prescriber’s signature were missing, I would not guess or fill them in myself. The pharmacist would contact the prescriber to verify or obtain the information as the law and pharmacy policy allow, document the clarification and hold the prescription until it is complete; some problems, such as a missing signature on a Schedule II prescription, require a new valid prescription.'},sources:[RULES,FDA_LAWS,DEA_SCHED,DEA_DIV,TJC_DNU,ISMP_ABBR]};

export const R3={title:'Prescriptions, abbreviations and filling a prescription (Skill VI)',hook:'“Amoxicillin 500 mg, i cap po tid × 10 d, disp #30.” To a pharmacy technician, that line is a complete set of instructions. To a patient it is a code. Translating prescription language accurately—and turning it into a correctly filled, labeled bottle—is the heart of pharmacy work and Skill VI.',objectives:['Interpret common prescription abbreviations and Latin terms.','Calculate quantity and days’ supply from a prescription.','Use brand and generic names and decide whether substitution is allowed.','Fill a prescription following HOSA Skill VI.'],sections:[
['Parts of a prescription',[
'A prescription has several parts. The **superscription** is the symbol **Rx** (“take thou”) and the patient information. The **inscription** names the drug, strength and dosage form. The **subscription** tells the pharmacy how much to dispense (**disp #30**). The **signa** or **sig** gives the patient’s directions, which become the label. The prescriber also indicates **refills**, whether a **generic substitution** is permitted and signs the prescription.',
'Most prescriptions now arrive electronically (**e-prescribing**), which reduces handwriting errors and is required for controlled substances in many states. Prescriptions may also be written, faxed, or phoned in by the prescriber to a pharmacist. In most states a non-controlled prescription is valid for **one year** from the date written, which is why Skill VI asks you to confirm the date is within the past year.',
match('Prescription parts',[['Superscription','Rx symbol and patient information'],['Inscription','Drug name, strength and dosage form'],['Subscription','Quantity to dispense'],['Signa (sig)','Directions for the patient'],['Refills','How many times it may be refilled'],['Product selection','Whether a generic may be substituted']])]],
['Common abbreviations',[
'The official HOSA reference for abbreviations is the Drugs.com list. Learn the core set: **po** (by mouth), **pr** (rectally), **sl** (under the tongue), **IM** (intramuscular), **IV** (intravenous), **subcut** (under the skin; avoid SC/SQ), **qd** is a Do Not Use abbreviation—write **daily**; **bid** (twice daily), **tid** (three times daily), **qid** (four times daily), **q4h** (every 4 hours), **prn** (as needed), **ac** (before meals), **pc** (after meals), **hs** (at bedtime; error-prone), **stat** (immediately) and **NPO** (nothing by mouth).',
'Dosage-form and measurement abbreviations include **tab**, **cap**, **susp** (suspension), **sol** (solution), **ung** (ointment), **gtt** (drops), **mL**, **mg**, **mcg** (avoid µg), **g**, **tsp** and **tbsp**. Directions to the pharmacy include **disp** (dispense), **sig** (write on label), **DAW** (dispense as written) and **NR** (no refills). Lowercase Roman numerals often show quantity: **i** = 1, **ii** = 2, **iii** = 3.',
{t:'table',caption:'Abbreviations to know',head:['Abbreviation','Meaning','Abbreviation','Meaning'],rows:[['po','by mouth','ac','before meals'],['bid','twice a day','pc','after meals'],['tid','three times a day','prn','as needed'],['qid','four times a day','hs','at bedtime (error-prone)'],['q6h','every 6 hours','stat','immediately'],['sl','under the tongue','gtt','drops'],['DAW','dispense as written','NPO','nothing by mouth']]},
match('Decode the sig',[['i tab po bid','Take 1 tablet by mouth twice a day'],['ii caps po q6h prn pain','Take 2 capsules by mouth every 6 hours as needed for pain'],['i gtt OU tid','Instill 1 drop in both eyes three times a day (write “both eyes”)'],['i tab sl prn chest pain','Dissolve 1 tablet under the tongue as needed for chest pain'],['10 mL po ac','Take 10 mL by mouth before meals'],['apply ung bid','Apply ointment twice a day']])]],
['Quantity and days’ supply',[
'Insurance plans and the law often depend on the **days’ supply**—how long the medicine will last. Days’ supply = **quantity dispensed ÷ amount used per day**. For “i tab po tid, disp #90,” the patient uses 3 tablets a day, so 90 ÷ 3 = **30 days**. For a liquid, “5 mL po bid, disp 100 mL,” the patient uses 10 mL a day, so 100 ÷ 10 = **10 days**. If you are asked to calculate the quantity, reverse it: quantity = dose per day × days.',
'Know common household conversions: **1 tsp = 5 mL**, **1 tbsp = 15 mL**, **1 fl oz = 30 mL** (about 29.57 mL), so **1 fl oz = 2 tbsp**. A pint is about **473 mL** (often rounded to 480 mL in pharmacy). Always use an oral syringe or calibrated cup rather than a kitchen spoon.',
{t:'example',title:'Calculate the quantity',prompt:'Amoxicillin 500 mg, i cap po tid × 10 days. How many capsules should be dispensed? (Invented for practice.)',steps:['Capsules per day: 1 × 3 = 3','Days: 10','3 × 10 = 30'],answer:'Dispense 30 capsules.'},
{t:'w',kind:'dosecalc',title:'Tablets, liquids and conversions',types:['tablets','liquid','convert'],prompt:'Write the setup with units before you check. New problems appear every time.'}]],
['Brand, generic and substitution',[
'Every drug has a **chemical name**, a **generic (nonproprietary) name** and often one or more **brand (trade) names**. Generic names are written in lowercase (atorvastatin); brand names are capitalized (Lipitor). The FDA approves generics that are **therapeutically equivalent**—the same active ingredient, strength, dosage form and route, shown to be **bioequivalent**. The FDA’s **Orange Book** lists therapeutic equivalence ratings, and most states allow pharmacists to substitute an equivalent generic unless the prescriber writes **DAW** or “brand medically necessary.”',
'Generic names often share **stems** that tell you the drug class: **-pril** (ACE inhibitors), **-sartan** (ARBs), **-olol** (beta blockers), **-statin** (cholesterol drugs), **-prazole** (proton pump inhibitors), **-cillin** (penicillins) and **-azepam/-azolam** (benzodiazepines). Scenarios in the event use both brand and generic names, so practice pairs such as lisinopril/Zestril, metformin/Glucophage, levothyroxine/Synthroid and sertraline/Zoloft.',
match('Brand and generic',[['Lipitor','atorvastatin'],['Zestril','lisinopril'],['Glucophage','metformin'],['Synthroid','levothyroxine'],['Zoloft','sertraline'],['Norvasc','amlodipine'],['Prilosec','omeprazole'],['Amoxil','amoxicillin']])]],
['Filling a prescription: Skill VI',[
'Skill VI has 19 steps in **5 minutes**. **1.** Obtain the prescription. **2.** Review it for legality and correctness: verify the **patient name by asking the patient**, confirm the **date is within the past year**, and verify the **address** and **date of birth**. **3.** Review the order with the pharmacist: correct **dosage form** and **strength**. **4.** Decide whether **generic substitution** is allowed. **5.** Perform hand hygiene. **6.** Obtain the correct medication. **7.** Check the **expiration date**. **8.** Calculate the number of tablets or capsules.',
'**9.** Use the **counting tray and spatula** to count—usually by fives. **10.** **Recount**. **11.** Choose the most appropriate **container** (type and size; amber vials protect from light). **12.** Get the correct **label**, rechecking directions and strength. **13.** Add **auxiliary labels** such as “take with food” or “shake well.” **14.** Submit to the pharmacist for the **final check**. **15.** Never touch the medication directly. **16.** Clean the counting tray and spatula with an **alcohol swab**. **17.** Return equipment. **18.** Hand hygiene. **19.** Communicate professionally throughout.',
order('Skill VI order',['Obtain and review the prescription','Verify name, date within 1 year, address and DOB','Review dosage form and strength with the pharmacist','Decide whether a generic is allowed','Hand hygiene, then get the drug and check expiration','Calculate, count by fives with the tray and spatula, and recount','Choose the container, label and auxiliary labels','Submit for final check; clean the tray; hand hygiene'])]],
['Auxiliary labels and error prevention',[
'**Auxiliary labels** add warnings the main label does not cover: “Take with food,” “May cause drowsiness,” “Do not drink alcohol,” “Shake well,” “Refrigerate,” “For external use only,” “Do not crush or chew,” “Finish all of this medication” and “Avoid prolonged sunlight.” Choose labels that match the drug—for example, “Shake well” on a suspension, “Finish all” on an antibiotic and “Do not crush” on an extended-release tablet.',
'Errors often happen at handoffs, so build habits: compare the stock bottle’s **NDC number** with the label, read the label **three times** (when you pull the stock bottle, when you count and when you return it), keep only one prescription in your work area at a time and never skip the recount. Scanning barcodes adds another layer of checking.',
sort('Match the auxiliary label',['Shake well','Do not crush or chew','Finish all of this medication','May cause drowsiness'],[['Amoxicillin suspension',0],['Metoprolol extended-release tablets',1],['Azithromycin tablets',2],['Hydroxyzine',3],['Nifedipine ER',1],['Cetirizine syrup',0]])]]
],takeaways:['A prescription includes the superscription, inscription, subscription, sig, refills, product selection and signature; non-controlled prescriptions are usually valid for one year.','Know po, bid, tid, qid, prn, ac, pc, sl, gtt, DAW and household conversions (1 tsp 5 mL, 1 tbsp 15 mL, 1 fl oz 30 mL = 2 tbsp); days’ supply = quantity ÷ daily use.','Skill VI: verify the patient and date, review form and strength, check substitution and expiration, count and recount with tray and spatula, label, auxiliary labels, final check and clean the tray.'],terms:[['Sig','The directions for the patient written on the prescription label.'],['Days’ supply','How many days a dispensed quantity will last.'],['Auxiliary label','An extra warning sticker added to a medication container.']],mcqs:[q('If a patient is to receive 1 ounce of an OTC medication, how many tablespoons should be given?','2 tablespoons','1 tablespoon','3 tablespoons','4 tablespoons','1 fl oz = 30 mL = 2 × 15 mL. HOSA sample question.'),q('What does “tid” mean?','Three times a day','Twice a day','Every three hours','Three tablets','From the Latin ter in die.'),q('A sig reads i tab po bid, disp #60. What is the days’ supply?','30 days','60 days','15 days','120 days','60 ÷ 2 = 30.'),q('What does DAW on a prescription mean?','Dispense as written—no generic substitution','Dispense after waking','Daily as warranted','Do any work','Brand is required.'),q('Which tool is used to count tablets in Skill VI?','Counting tray and spatula','Bare hands','Mortar and pestle','Graduated cylinder','Never touch the drug.'),q('Which auxiliary label belongs on an amoxicillin suspension?','Shake well','Do not crush','For external use only','Avoid sunlight','Suspensions settle.'),q('How long is a typical non-controlled prescription valid in most states?','One year','One week','Six months','Forever','Skill VI checks the date is within the past year.'),q('Generic name stem -statin indicates:','A cholesterol-lowering drug','An antibiotic','A beta blocker','A benzodiazepine','Atorvastatin, simvastatin.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient presents a prescription: “Cephalexin 500 mg caps, i cap po qid × 7 days.” Explain how you would interpret and fill it following Skill VI, including the quantity, the auxiliary labels you would choose and the checks you would perform. (Invented for practice.)',rubric:[['Interpretation',['1 capsule','by mouth','four times','7 days','28']],['Verification',['name','date','one year','address','date of birth','pharmacist','strength','generic']],['Filling',['expiration','counting tray','spatula','recount','container','label']],['Auxiliary and final',['finish','auxiliary','final check','clean','alcohol','hand']]],model:'The sig means take 1 capsule by mouth four times a day for 7 days, so the patient uses 4 capsules a day and I calculate 4 × 7 = 28 capsules. I obtain the prescription and review it for legality and correctness: I verify the patient’s name by asking the patient, confirm the date was written within the past year and verify the address and date of birth. I review the dosage form (capsules) and strength (500 mg) with the pharmacist and check whether generic substitution is allowed. I wash my hands, pull the correct stock bottle, check that it matches by name, strength and NDC, and check the expiration date. Using the counting tray and spatula, I count 28 capsules in fives without touching them and recount. I choose an appropriately sized amber vial with a child-resistant cap, attach the correct label after rechecking the directions and strength and add auxiliary labels such as “Finish all of this medication unless otherwise directed.” I submit the prescription to the pharmacist for the final check, clean the tray and spatula with an alcohol swab, return equipment and wash my hands, communicating professionally throughout.'},sources:[RULES,ABBR,TJC_DNU,FDA_ORANGE,MEDLINE_DRUGS]};

export const R4={title:'Dosage forms, routes and pharmacokinetics',hook:'The same drug can be a tablet, a liquid, a patch, an inhaler or an injection. The form changes how fast the drug works, how long it lasts and who can use it. A child who cannot swallow pills needs a suspension; a patient in an emergency needs an IV. This lesson explains dosage forms, routes of administration and what the body does to a drug.',objectives:['Compare solid, liquid, semisolid and other dosage forms.','Describe routes of administration and their advantages and limits.','Explain absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion (pharmacokinetics).','Recognize forms that must not be crushed and why.'],sections:[
['Solid dosage forms',[
'**Tablets** are compressed powders. Some are **scored** so they can be split. **Film-coated** tablets are easier to swallow; **enteric-coated (EC)** tablets resist stomach acid and dissolve in the intestine, protecting the stomach or the drug. **Extended-release** forms (ER, XR, XL, SR, CR, LA) release the drug slowly over many hours, so they are taken less often. **Chewable** and **orally disintegrating tablets (ODT)** help people who have trouble swallowing. **Sublingual** and **buccal** tablets dissolve under the tongue or in the cheek and are absorbed directly into the blood.',
'**Capsules** are gelatin or vegetable shells filled with powder, beads or liquid. **Lozenges** (troches) dissolve slowly in the mouth. **Powders** and **granules** are mixed with water or food. **Do not crush or chew** enteric-coated, extended-release, sublingual or many hazardous drugs—crushing an extended-release tablet can release the whole day’s dose at once. The ISMP keeps a “Do Not Crush” list.',
sort('Can it usually be crushed?',['Usually okay to crush (check references)','Do not crush'],[['Plain acetaminophen tablet',0],['Metoprolol succinate ER',1],['Enteric-coated aspirin',1],['Nifedipine XL',1],['Plain lisinopril tablet',0],['Sublingual nitroglycerin',1]])]],
['Liquid, semisolid and other forms',[
'**Solutions** have the drug completely dissolved. **Syrups** are sweetened solutions, **elixirs** contain alcohol, and **tinctures** are alcoholic plant extracts. **Suspensions** contain undissolved particles that settle, so they need **“shake well”** labels; many antibiotic suspensions are **reconstituted** with water at the pharmacy and refrigerated. **Emulsions** are mixtures of oil and water held together by an emulsifier.',
'**Semisolids** for the skin include **ointments** (greasy, good for dry skin), **creams** (water-based, absorb easily), **gels** and **lotions**. **Suppositories** melt at body temperature for rectal or vaginal use. **Transdermal patches** deliver drug through the skin for days (fentanyl, nicotine, clonidine). **Inhalers**—metered-dose inhalers (MDI), dry powder inhalers (DPI) and nebulizers—deliver drug to the lungs. **Ophthalmic** products go in the eye, **otic** in the ear and **nasal** sprays in the nose.',
match('Match the form',[['Suspension','Particles that settle—shake well'],['Elixir','Sweetened solution with alcohol'],['Ointment','Greasy semisolid for dry skin'],['Transdermal patch','Delivers drug through the skin for days'],['Suppository','Melts at body temperature'],['Dry powder inhaler','Breath-activated powder to the lungs'],['Enteric-coated tablet','Dissolves in the intestine'],['ODT','Dissolves on the tongue']])]],
['Routes of administration',[
'**Enteral** routes use the digestive tract: **oral** (po) is convenient and inexpensive but slower and affected by food and stomach acid; **sublingual** and **buccal** avoid the stomach; **rectal** helps when a patient is vomiting or cannot swallow; feeding tubes (NG, G-tube) deliver drugs directly. **Parenteral** routes bypass the digestive tract: **intravenous (IV)** acts immediately with 100% availability; **intramuscular (IM)** suits vaccines and depot drugs; **subcutaneous (subcut)** suits insulin and heparin; **intradermal (ID)** is used for TB skin tests.',
'**Topical** and **other** routes include the skin, eye, ear, nose, lungs and vagina. Inhaled drugs act directly in the lungs with fewer whole-body effects. The route chosen depends on how fast the drug must work, whether the patient can swallow, the drug’s stability in the stomach and whether a local or systemic effect is wanted.',
{t:'table',caption:'Routes compared',head:['Route','Onset','Example use'],rows:[['IV','Immediate','Emergency drugs, fluids, many antibiotics'],['Sublingual','Minutes','Nitroglycerin for chest pain'],['Inhalation','Minutes','Albuterol for asthma'],['IM','Minutes to hours','Vaccines, some antibiotics'],['Subcut','Slower, steady','Insulin, heparin'],['Oral','30–60 minutes or more','Most chronic medicines'],['Transdermal','Hours; lasts days','Nicotine, fentanyl patches']]}]],
['Pharmacokinetics: what the body does to a drug',[
'**Pharmacokinetics** is the study of how a drug moves through the body: **absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion (ADME)**. **Absorption** is movement from the site of administration into the blood. Oral drugs absorbed from the intestine travel first to the liver, which may break down much of the drug before it reaches the body—the **first-pass effect**. That is why some drugs, such as nitroglycerin, are given sublingually. **Bioavailability** is the fraction of a dose that reaches circulation unchanged; IV drugs have 100% bioavailability.',
'**Distribution** carries the drug to tissues; some drugs bind to plasma proteins such as albumin, and only the unbound drug is active. **Metabolism**, mostly in the **liver** by enzymes such as **cytochrome P450**, converts drugs to forms the body can remove; some drugs (prodrugs) become active only after metabolism. **Excretion** removes drugs, mostly through the **kidneys** in urine, and also through bile, lungs and sweat. The **half-life** is the time for the drug level to fall by half; it takes about **4–5 half-lives** to reach a steady state or to clear a drug. **Pharmacodynamics** is the opposite view—what the drug does to the body.',
order('ADME in order',['Absorption into the blood','Distribution to tissues','Metabolism (mostly liver)','Excretion (mostly kidneys)']),
{t:'w',kind:'pk',title:'Pharmacokinetics simulator',prompt:'Change the dosing interval and the half-life, then switch from IV to oral. Watch how peaks, troughs and time to steady state change.'}]],
['Factors that change drug response',[
'**Age** matters: infants have immature livers and kidneys, and older adults often have reduced kidney function, more body fat and multiple medicines (**polypharmacy**). **Weight**, **genetics** (pharmacogenomics), **pregnancy**, **liver and kidney disease** and **diet** also change drug levels. Grapefruit juice blocks an intestinal enzyme and can raise levels of some statins and calcium channel blockers. Dairy and antacids bind tetracycline and ciprofloxacin.',
'**Drug interactions** occur when one drug changes another’s effect—warfarin with many antibiotics or NSAIDs can increase bleeding. **Adverse effects** range from mild side effects to **allergic reactions** and **anaphylaxis**. The pharmacist reviews every new prescription for these problems, and technicians help by keeping the **patient profile** complete with all prescription, OTC and herbal products and allergies.',
chk('Why do older adults often need lower doses of drugs cleared by the kidneys?',['Kidney function often declines with age','Older adults absorb nothing','Their livers grow larger','They have more water in the body'],'Reduced clearance raises drug levels.')]],
['Practice: choosing a form',[
'Pharmacy technicians help match a product to the patient, under the pharmacist’s direction. A toddler may need a **flavored suspension**; a patient with nausea may need an **ODT** or a **suppository**; a patient who struggles with daily pills may benefit from a **once-daily extended-release** form; someone with severe asthma needs an **inhaler** with a spacer. If a prescriber orders a form that seems wrong for the patient—such as crushing an ER tablet for a feeding tube—the pharmacist contacts the prescriber.',
kase('Wrong form?',{a:{text:'A caregiver asks if they can crush their father’s Toprol XL (metoprolol succinate ER) to mix in applesauce. What is the best response from the technician?',choices:[['Explain that you need to check with the pharmacist, because ER tablets usually should not be crushed','b','Correct. Crushing can release the whole dose at once; the pharmacist will advise.',true],['Say yes, all tablets can be crushed','b','Extended-release forms generally must not be crushed.',false]]},b:{text:'The pharmacist suggests asking the prescriber about a different option. Which form might work for someone who cannot swallow tablets?',choices:[['A liquid, ODT or other form the prescriber approves','c','Correct. Alternatives exist for many drugs.',true],['Chewing the ER tablet','c','Chewing has the same problem as crushing.',false]]},c:{text:'The prescriber changes the order, and the pharmacist counsels the caregiver. Problem solved safely.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Solid forms include tablets (scored, film, EC, ER, ODT, SL) and capsules; never crush EC, ER or SL forms without the pharmacist’s approval.','Liquids (solutions, syrups, elixirs, suspensions, emulsions), semisolids and other forms suit different patients; routes are enteral, parenteral (IV, IM, subcut, ID) and topical/inhaled.','Pharmacokinetics is ADME; first-pass metabolism reduces oral bioavailability; half-life sets dosing; age, organ function, genetics and interactions change response.'],terms:[['Pharmacokinetics','The study of drug absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion.'],['First-pass effect','Breakdown of an oral drug by the liver before it reaches the body.'],['Half-life','The time for a drug’s concentration to fall by half.']],mcqs:[q('What is the term for the study of the movement of a drug through body systems, including absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion?','Pharmacokinetics','Pharmacology','Therapeutics','Pharmacogenomics','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which dosage form needs a “shake well” label?','Suspension','Solution','Tablet','Capsule','Particles settle.'),q('Which route has 100% bioavailability?','Intravenous','Oral','Rectal','Transdermal','It enters the blood directly.'),q('Why is nitroglycerin given sublingually?','To avoid first-pass metabolism and act quickly','Because it tastes good','To make it last a week','Because it cannot be absorbed anywhere else','Fast onset for chest pain.'),q('Where are most drugs metabolized?','Liver','Kidneys','Lungs','Skin','Kidneys mainly excrete.'),q('Which tablet should NOT be crushed?','Extended-release tablet','Plain scored tablet','Chewable tablet','Plain acetaminophen tablet','Crushing releases the full dose.'),q('About how many half-lives does it take to clear most of a drug?','4–5','1','10–20','0.5','Same for reaching steady state.'),q('Which route is used for a TB skin test?','Intradermal','IV','Oral','Rectal','Just under the top layers of skin.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare three routes of administration for the same drug and explain how pharmacokinetics (ADME) explains their differences in onset and dose.',rubric:[['Routes described',['iv','oral','sublingual','im','subcut','transdermal']],['Absorption and bioavailability',['absorption','bioavailability','100%','first-pass','liver']],['Distribution, metabolism, excretion',['distribution','protein','metabolism','cytochrome','excretion','kidney']],['Patient factors',['age','kidney','liver','half-life','interaction']]],model:'An IV dose goes directly into the blood, so absorption is not needed, bioavailability is 100% and the onset is immediate. An oral tablet must dissolve and be absorbed in the intestine, then passes through the liver, where the first-pass effect may break down part of the dose; the onset is slower, so an oral dose may need to be larger than an IV dose for the same effect. A sublingual tablet is absorbed through the mouth’s blood vessels into the circulation, avoiding the first pass, so it acts within minutes—useful for nitroglycerin. After absorption, every route shares the same steps: distribution to the tissues, where protein binding limits how much drug is active; metabolism, mostly by liver cytochrome P450 enzymes; and excretion, mostly by the kidneys. The half-life determines how often the drug is given, and it takes about four to five half-lives to reach steady state. Age, kidney or liver disease, genetics and drug interactions can change these steps, so the pharmacist may adjust the dose.'},sources:[RULES,OSP_PHARM,MEDLINE_DRUGS,ISMP_ABBR]};

export const R5={title:'Pharmacy calculations: conversions, concentrations and dilutions',hook:'A pharmacist asks you to make 500 mL of a 2% solution from a 10% stock. Another order asks for 0.25 mg of a drug that comes as 0.5 mg/mL. Every one of these problems has the same answer to “how do I avoid a mistake?”: write the units, set up the problem and check whether the answer makes sense. Concentrations and dilutions are 10% of the test.',objectives:['Convert between metric and household units.','Express concentrations as percent, ratio strength and mg/mL.','Use C₁V₁ = C₂V₂ for dilutions and the alligation method for mixing strengths.','Calculate doses, including weight-based doses.'],sections:[
['The metric system and conversions',[
'Pharmacy uses the **metric system**. **1 kilogram (kg) = 1,000 grams (g)**; **1 g = 1,000 milligrams (mg)**; **1 mg = 1,000 micrograms (mcg)**; **1 liter (L) = 1,000 milliliters (mL)**. To change a larger unit to a smaller one, multiply (the number gets bigger); to change a smaller unit to a larger one, divide. Household equivalents: **1 tsp = 5 mL**, **1 tbsp = 15 mL**, **1 fl oz = 30 mL** (29.57 mL), **1 cup = 240 mL**, **1 pint ≈ 473 mL**, **1 kg = 2.2 lb** and **1 grain ≈ 65 mg** (64.8 mg).',
'Use **dimensional analysis**: write what you have, multiply by conversion factors arranged so the unwanted units cancel and finish with the unit you want. For example, 2 tbsp × 15 mL/tbsp = 30 mL. Always apply the zero rules: **0.5 mg**, not .5 mg, and **5 mg**, not 5.0 mg.',
{t:'table',caption:'Equivalents to memorize',head:['Unit','Equivalent'],rows:[['1 g','1,000 mg'],['1 mg','1,000 mcg'],['1 L','1,000 mL'],['1 tsp','5 mL'],['1 tbsp','15 mL (3 tsp)'],['1 fl oz','30 mL (2 tbsp)'],['1 kg','2.2 lb']]},
{t:'w',kind:'mathlab',mode:'conversion',title:'Explore unit conversion factors'}]],
['Percent and ratio strength',[
'Concentrations show how much drug is in a given amount of product. **Percent weight-in-volume (% w/v)** means **grams per 100 mL**: a 5% dextrose solution has 5 g of dextrose in every 100 mL. **% w/w** means grams per 100 g (used for ointments and creams), and **% v/v** means mL per 100 mL (used for alcohol solutions). Normal saline is 0.9% sodium chloride: 0.9 g per 100 mL, or 9 g per liter.',
'**Ratio strength** expresses weak concentrations as 1 part in a number of parts: epinephrine 1:1,000 means 1 g in 1,000 mL, which equals **1 mg/mL** or 0.1%. Convert ratio strength to percent by dividing and multiplying by 100: 1 ÷ 1,000 × 100 = 0.1%. Concentrations can also be written as **mg/mL**, such as amoxicillin 250 mg/5 mL.',
{t:'example',title:'How much drug is in the bag?',prompt:'How many grams of dextrose are in 500 mL of D5W (5% w/v)? (Invented for practice.)',steps:['5% w/v means 5 g per 100 mL.','5 g/100 mL × 500 mL = 25 g.'],answer:'25 g of dextrose.'},
match('Match the concentration',[['5% w/v','5 g per 100 mL'],['0.9% NaCl','0.9 g per 100 mL'],['1:1,000','1 mg/mL (0.1%)'],['1% w/w cream','1 g per 100 g'],['70% v/v alcohol','70 mL per 100 mL'],['250 mg/5 mL','50 mg per mL']])]],
['Dose calculations',[
'The basic formula is **dose desired ÷ dose on hand × quantity = amount to give** (D/H × Q). If an order calls for 0.25 mg and the vial contains 0.5 mg/mL, then 0.25 ÷ 0.5 × 1 mL = **0.5 mL**. Ratio-proportion works the same way: 0.5 mg/1 mL = 0.25 mg/x mL, so x = 0.5 mL. Check your answer: you need half the amount in 1 mL, so half a milliliter makes sense.',
'**Weight-based doses** use mg/kg. Convert pounds to kilograms first (divide by 2.2), multiply by the dose per kilogram and then decide how to divide the total into doses. For a 44-lb child prescribed 10 mg/kg/day divided twice daily: 44 ÷ 2.2 = 20 kg; 20 × 10 = 200 mg/day; 200 ÷ 2 = **100 mg per dose**. Always confirm whether the dose is per dose or per day.',
{t:'example',title:'Weight-based dose',prompt:'Amoxicillin suspension 250 mg/5 mL. A 33-lb child is prescribed 45 mg/kg/day divided every 12 hours. How many mL per dose? (Invented for practice.)',steps:['33 lb ÷ 2.2 = 15 kg','15 kg × 45 mg/kg/day = 675 mg/day','675 ÷ 2 doses = 337.5 mg per dose','337.5 mg × 5 mL/250 mg = 6.75 mL'],answer:'6.75 mL per dose (the pharmacist confirms the rounding).'},
{t:'w',kind:'dosecalc',title:'Weight-based drill',types:['weight'],prompt:'Convert pounds to kilograms first, then decide whether the dose is per day or per dose.'}]],
['Dilutions: C₁V₁ = C₂V₂',[
'When you dilute a concentrated **stock** solution, the amount of drug stays the same while the volume increases. The formula is **C₁ × V₁ = C₂ × V₂**: the stock concentration times the stock volume equals the final concentration times the final volume. To make **500 mL of 2%** from a **10% stock**: 10% × V₁ = 2% × 500 mL, so V₁ = **100 mL** of stock, plus **400 mL** of diluent.',
'You can also use the formula to find the new concentration after adding diluent. If 50 mL of a 20% solution is diluted to 200 mL, then 20% × 50 = C₂ × 200, so C₂ = **5%**. Check: diluting four times should make the concentration one-fourth as strong. The units of C₁ and C₂ must match, as must V₁ and V₂.',
{t:'w',kind:'mathlab',mode:'dilution',title:'Practice a dilution with C₁V₁ = C₂V₂'},
chk('You need 1 L of 0.45% saline from 0.9% saline. How much 0.9% do you use?',['500 mL','450 mL','900 mL','100 mL'],'0.9 × V₁ = 0.45 × 1,000; V₁ = 500 mL, plus 500 mL sterile water.')]],
['Alligation',[
'**Alligation** finds how much of a stronger and a weaker product to mix to get a strength in between. Draw a tic-tac-toe grid: put the **higher strength** at the top left, the **lower strength** at the bottom left and the **desired strength** in the center. Subtract diagonally (ignore negative signs): the **difference between the desired and lower** strengths gives the **parts of the higher** strength, and the **difference between the higher and desired** gives the **parts of the lower**.',
'Example: make 100 g of **15%** ointment from **20%** and **5%** ointments. Parts of 20% = 15 − 5 = **10**; parts of 5% = 20 − 15 = **5**; total = 15 parts. 20% needed = 10/15 × 100 g = **66.7 g**; 5% needed = 5/15 × 100 g = **33.3 g**. Plain diluent such as water or petrolatum counts as **0%**.',
{t:'example',title:'Alligation with a diluent',prompt:'How much 70% isopropyl alcohol and water (0%) make 210 mL of 50% alcohol? (Invented for practice.)',steps:['Parts of 70%: 50 − 0 = 50','Parts of water: 70 − 50 = 20','Total parts: 70','70%: 50/70 × 210 = 150 mL; water: 20/70 × 210 = 60 mL'],answer:'150 mL of 70% alcohol plus 60 mL of water.'},
order('Alligation steps',['Write higher strength top left, lower strength bottom left','Write the desired strength in the center','Subtract diagonally to find parts','Add the parts for a total','Multiply each fraction by the total amount needed','Check that the amounts add up to the total'])]],
['Check your work',[
'Most calculation errors come from wrong units, misplaced decimals or forgetting whether a dose is per day or per dose. Estimate first: if you are diluting, the final concentration must be lower; if a dose is half the concentration per mL, the volume must be less than 1 mL. Write every unit, keep your work neat and round only the final answer as directed.',
{t:'callout',kind:'mistake',title:'Common traps',text:'Mixing up mg and mcg (a 1,000-fold error); forgetting to convert pounds to kilograms; treating a daily dose as a single dose; adding the leading zero incorrectly; and rounding in the middle of a problem instead of at the end.'},
kase('Dilution check',{a:{text:'You calculate that you need 1,000 mL of a 10% stock to make 500 mL of a 2% solution. What should you notice?',choices:[['The stock volume cannot be larger than the final volume—recheck the setup','b','Correct. A dilution always uses less stock than the final volume.',true],['Proceed; the math is the math','b','Estimating catches impossible answers.',false]]},b:{text:'You redo it: 10 × V₁ = 2 × 500, so V₁ = 100 mL. How much diluent do you add?',choices:[['400 mL','c','Correct. 500 − 100 = 400 mL.',true],['500 mL','c','The final volume is 500 mL total.',false]]},c:{text:'You verify the setup with the pharmacist. Correct and checked.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Metric: 1 g = 1,000 mg = 1,000,000 mcg; household: 1 tsp 5 mL, 1 tbsp 15 mL, 1 fl oz 30 mL; 1 kg = 2.2 lb.','% w/v = g per 100 mL; ratio 1:1,000 = 1 mg/mL; D/H × Q gives the amount to give; convert lb to kg for weight-based doses.','Dilution: C₁V₁ = C₂V₂; alligation subtracts diagonally to find parts; estimate and check units before trusting an answer.'],terms:[['% w/v','Grams of solute per 100 mL of solution.'],['Ratio strength','A concentration written as 1 part in a number of parts, such as 1:1,000.'],['Alligation','A method for mixing two strengths to make a strength in between.']],mcqs:[q('How many mg are in 0.25 g?','250 mg','25 mg','2.5 mg','2,500 mg','Multiply by 1,000.'),q('How many grams of NaCl are in 1 L of 0.9% NaCl?','9 g','0.9 g','90 g','0.09 g','0.9 g per 100 mL × 10.'),q('Epinephrine 1:1,000 is equal to:','1 mg/mL','10 mg/mL','0.1 mg/mL','1 g/mL','1 g per 1,000 mL.'),q('A vial contains 40 mg/mL. How many mL provide 100 mg?','2.5 mL','4 mL','0.4 mL','25 mL','100 ÷ 40 = 2.5.'),q('How much 20% stock is needed for 400 mL of 5%?','100 mL','80 mL','200 mL','50 mL','20 × V₁ = 5 × 400.'),q('A child weighs 66 lb. What is the weight in kg?','30 kg','145 kg','33 kg','60 kg','66 ÷ 2.2.'),q('Using alligation, how many parts of 30% are needed to make 20% from 30% and 10%?','10 parts','20 parts','5 parts','30 parts','20 − 10 = 10 parts of 30%; 30 − 20 = 10 parts of 10%.'),q('How many mL are in 3 teaspoons?','15 mL','5 mL','30 mL','3 mL','3 × 5 mL = 1 tbsp.')],frq:{prompt:'The pharmacist asks you to prepare 240 mL of a 1:500 solution from a 5% stock solution. Show how you convert the ratio strength, set up the dilution and determine how much stock and diluent you need. (Invented for practice.)',rubric:[['Convert ratio strength',['1:500','0.2%','1 ÷ 500','100']],['Set up dilution',['c1v1','c2v2','5%','240']],['Solve',['9.6','mL','stock','230.4','diluent']],['Check',['less','estimate','units','pharmacist','verify']]],model:'First I convert the ratio strength to a percent: 1:500 means 1 g in 500 mL, so 1 ÷ 500 × 100 = 0.2%. Next I use C₁V₁ = C₂V₂ with matching units: 5% × V₁ = 0.2% × 240 mL. Solving, V₁ = (0.2 × 240) ÷ 5 = 48 ÷ 5 = 9.6 mL of the 5% stock. The diluent is the final volume minus the stock: 240 − 9.6 = 230.4 mL. To check, the final concentration should be much weaker than 5%, and 0.2% is 1/25 of 5%; 240 mL ÷ 25 = 9.6 mL, which matches. The amount of stock is far less than the final volume, as a dilution should be. I would write each unit, keep the leading zero in any decimals and have the pharmacist verify my calculation before preparing the solution.'},sources:[RULES,ABBR,OSP_PHARM]};
